<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RQFlinch - the "shrug it off" hit reaction.

  Selected instead of the vanilla reaction whenever the RQNoFlinch animation
  variable is true on the zombie. RQFlinch.lua (client) owns that variable and
  RQPoise decides who gets it; the variable name is defined in one place in
  RQFlinch.lua and MUST match the condition below. A mismatch fails silently -
  this node simply never wins and flinches look normal. test_rqflinch.lua reads
  this file and compares, because that is the half a Lua test can actually
  prove.

  WHY A NODE AND NOT A TRANSITION. We cannot stop the zombie ENTERING the
  hitreaction state: those transitions live in media/actiongroups, which
  ActionGroup.load() reads from the base install only (getMediaFile plus
  listFiles), with no mod path. AnimSets ARE mod-loadable - AnimationSet:54
  goes through resolveAllDirectories -> walkGameAndModFiles. So we let the
  state be entered and make it cost nothing.

  WHY THIS ACTUALLY ENDS THE STATE, verified 2026-08-25. The exit transition
  actiongroups/zombie/hitreaction/to_idle.xml is:
      <eventOccurred>ActiveAnimFinishing</eventOccurred>
  - an ANIMATION event, not a flag test. So shortening the animation genuinely
  shortens the state. This is the load-bearing fact of the whole feature, and
  it is why the two earlier flag-clearing attempts could never have worked.
  Contrast staggerback/to_idle.xml, which exits on a stateEventDelayTimer
  instead and is therefore immune to speed scaling - RQFlinch.releaseStagger
  handles that lane separately.

  WHY m_ConditionPriority. AnimNode.compareSelectionConditions (AnimNode.java
  :287-301) orders by abstract-ness, then m_ConditionPriority, then condition
  COUNT, and AnimState.addNode (:78-91) inserts highest-first. Winning on count
  alone would break the day vanilla adds a condition to a sibling node, so the
  priority is stated outright. Vanilla zombie nodes leave it at the 0 default
  (AnimNode.java:45-46), so 100 wins without modifying any of them.

  m_SpeedScale is what makes this work. It is a STRING field
  (AnimNode.java:93-94 - it also accepts a variable name), and the reaction
  animation still plays, at twenty times speed, so it is over inside a frame or
  two and the zombie is back to acting. It reads as a twitch rather than as
  nothing happening, which is the intent - a Bulwark should look like it
  noticed and did not care.

  THE EVENTS. ZombieHitReactionState.animEvent (:103-109) turns "KnockDown"
  into setKnockedDown(true) plus setOnFloor(true), and "CancelKnockDown" into
  setKnockedDown(false). This node emits no KnockDown and cancels a pending
  one. SetState is carried over from vanilla ShotDefault.xml purely so the hit
  still sounds like a hit (:124-134).

  CORRECTED 2026-08-25 (floor-state enumeration). This paragraph used to say
  that "for gunfire the animation event IS the mechanism", and that the cancel
  here "is how a Bulwark becomes immune to being put on the floor without us
  fighting a flag race". Both were wrong, and the second was the one that
  mattered.

  The flag does not come from an animation event. IsoZombie.hitConsequences
  :3459 sets it in Java, at hit time, with no event involved:

      this.setKnockedDown(wielder.isCriticalHit() || this.isOnFloor()
                          || this.isAlwaysKnockedDown());

  (Client-side only - the surrounding gate needs GameClient.client and a local
  wielder - which is consistent with this whole file living in client/.)

  And the cancel does not demonstrably win the race. Vanilla's own concrete
  node for the same reaction string, Shot/ShotChestStepL.xml, x_extends
  ShotDefault.xml and so inherits exactly this CancelKnockDown(Start, true) -
  while actiongroups/zombie/hitreaction/to_knockeddown-shotChestL.xml requires
  <isTrue>bKnockedDown</isTrue> to leave this state. Vanilla ships six such
  knockdown states with full AnimSets, so it plainly expects them to be
  reached; that cannot be true if a Start-time cancel reliably beat the exit
  evaluation. Which wins on the entry frame is NOT settled by reading the
  graph, and this file will not claim it is. RQFlinch.observe records the
  action-context states a suppressed zombie actually passes through, so the
  next Mosaic session answers it with evidence instead.

  THE KNOCKDOWN LANE, and why six more nodes exist. Because the ordering is
  unresolved, the lane is covered rather than argued. A firearm crit from the
  LEFT or RIGHT is the case: CombatManager.resolveHitReaction:2330 takes the
  headshot early-return only on Rand.Next(4) == 0, so three crits in four fall
  through to the ordinary reaction table (:2350-2376) and can produce
  ShotChestStepL/R, ShotLegL/R or ShotShoulderL/R - the six strings the
  knockdown transitions compare against - while the same isCriticalHit() has
  already set bKnockedDown at :3459. Those six states now carry RQFlinch nodes
  of their own. If the cancel here wins they are never selected and cost
  nothing; if it loses they turn a full knockdown into the same two-frame
  twitch as every other lane. Correct either way, which is the point of
  covering rather than reasoning.

  The other four knockdown states - headLeft, headRight, headTop, uppercut -
  are NOT firearm-reachable: resolveHitReaction never returns HEAD_LEFT,
  HEAD_RIGHT, HEAD_TOP or UPPERCUT on any path, crit or not. They are melee
  shapes, and are deliberately left uncovered.

  ALL TEN are additionally gated by <isFalse>usePhysicHitReaction</isFalse>,
  which for a ranged weapon tracks RagdollSettingsManager.usePhysicHitReaction
  (CombatManager.java:3170-3177, called at :2385). On a server with ragdoll hit
  reactions enabled the whole knockdown lane is blocked by the engine and the
  ragdoll path runs instead. So whether these six ever get selected is a
  server-settings question as well as an ordering one - another reason to cover
  the lane rather than wait to observe it.

  NOT COVERED, ON PURPOSE: getup/ and getup-fromSitting/. The enumeration entry
  listed both, but neither is in the knockdown chain - getup/ serves crawlers,
  body-eaters and tripped sprinters, getup-fromSitting/ serves a seated zombie
  standing up. Worse, getup/'s three vanilla nodes are each conditioned
  (bGetUpFromCrawl, SprinterTripped, previousState == eatbody) and a node of
  ours at priority 100 would displace all three, replacing a crawler's
  stand-up with a generic one for every suppressed zombie. The floor chain uses
  getup-fromOnBack/ and getup-fromOnFront/, which are covered.
-->
<animNode>
	<m_Name>RQFlinchSuppressed</m_Name>
	<m_AnimName>Zombie_ShotChestL</m_AnimName>
	<m_Looped>false</m_Looped>
	<m_BlendTime>0.0</m_BlendTime>
	<m_BlendOutTime>0.0</m_BlendOutTime>
	<m_SpeedScale>20.00</m_SpeedScale>
	<m_ConditionPriority>100</m_ConditionPriority>
	<m_Conditions x_name="rqf1a2b3-0001-4000-8000-rftddirge0001">
		<m_Name>RQNoFlinch</m_Name>
		<m_Type>BOOL</m_Type>
		<m_Value>true</m_Value>
	</m_Conditions>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-0002-4000-8000-rftddirge0002">
		<m_EventName>CancelKnockDown</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue>true</m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
	<m_Events x_name="rqf1a2b3-0003-4000-8000-rftddirge0003">
		<m_EventName>SetState</m_EventName>
		<m_Time>Start</m_Time>
		<m_ParameterValue></m_ParameterValue>
	</m_Events>
</animNode>
